Wrap your chat UI in `AssistantRuntimeProvider`, imported from `@assistant-ui/react`. Pass it a `runtime` prop holding the runtime you created with a hook. Every component inside the provider reads from that runtime.

The smallest option is `useLocalRuntime`, which takes a `ChatModelAdapter`. Its `run` function receives `messages` and `abortSignal`, calls your API and returns the reply.

```tsx
const runtime = useLocalRuntime(MyModelAdapter);
return (
  <AssistantRuntimeProvider runtime={runtime}>
    {children}
  </AssistantRuntimeProvider>
);
```

The docs' example puts this in a `MyRuntimeProvider` component in a file marked `"use client"`. Most apps use a runtime hook built for their backend instead, such as `useChatRuntime` (Vercel AI SDK) or `useLangGraphRuntime` (LangGraph). The page doesn't describe any other props for the provider.

Pages: docs/concepts/runtime.md

To widen the column, edit your own copy of `thread.aui.tsx`, which you import from `@/components/assistant-ui/elements/thread.aui`. `Thread` sets the `--thread-max-width` CSS variable as an inline style on `ThreadPrimitive.Root`. Change its value there, for example:

```tsx
style={{
  ["--thread-max-width" as string]: "56rem",
}}
```

That snippet shows only the relevant line, so leave the other style entries in place. The same inline style also sets `--composer-bg`, `--composer-radius` and `--composer-padding`.

Adding a class from outside won't override it, because the value is set directly on the root rather than inherited. The documented `ThreadProps` are only `components` and `autoFocus`, so no width prop is listed.

The docs don't say what the default width is. They also don't explicitly say which elements use the variable, such as whether messages and the composer both widen.

Pages: elements/thread.md

In the one message component, use the `MessagePrimitive.Parts` children render function and pick components by tool name. The pages don't show this exact per-name branch; I've combined their render-function pattern with the `toolName` field:

```tsx
<MessagePrimitive.Parts>
  {({ part }) => {
    if (part.type === "text") return <MyText />;
    if (part.type === "tool-call") {
      if (part.toolName === "get_weather") return <Weather {...part} />;
      return part.toolUI ?? <ToolFallback {...part} />;
    }
    return null;
  }}
</MessagePrimitive.Parts>
```

`part.toolUI` is the registered UI, or `null`. Returning `null` still lets registered UIs render automatically; `<></>` suppresses them.

The documented inline option, `components={{ tools: { by_name: { get_weather: Weather } } }}`, still appears, but the `components` prop is **deprecated**. There, globally registered tools take precedence over `by_name`; `tools.Override` handles all tool calls.

For subtree scoping, the docs also show `AuiProvider` with `Tools({ toolkit })`. That's still registration, and they don't say how isolated it is.

Pages: docs/primitives/message.md, docs/tools/tool-ui.md

Use `useAuiEvent` in a component rendered under `AssistantRuntimeProvider`. It can return `null`, and its subscription is removed on unmount.

```tsx
useAuiEvent("thread.runEnd", ({ threadId }) => {
  // your code
});
```

This form only hears the selected thread. To include threads the user has switched away from, pass `{ scope: "*", event: "thread.runEnd" }` as the first argument, and use `threadId` to tell them apart.

`thread.runEnd` means a run ended. The docs don't say whether it also fires after cancellation or errors, so don't assume the reply succeeded. Separate `thread.cancelRun` and `message.error` events exist. The docs also don't say whether `runEnd` includes the reply message, or how it behaves with multi-step tool calls or approval pauses.

To display running status, use `useAuiState((s) => s.thread.isRunning)` instead. The AI SDK v7 page documents no client-side finish callback and no server-side completion hook.

Pages: docs/concepts/context-api.md, docs/guides/state-outside-the-thread.md, docs/runtimes/ai-sdk/v7.md
